codemap 9
tour feature-add-comment
kind flow
author ai
group features/gui
note The human, reading a tour or a change they did not write, at the moment the map does not explain something or the code looks wrong, and they cannot fix the note themselves because they do not know the answer. Goals: understanding a change you did not write takes less friction than in an editor; the map is the second channel beside the source, and it only gets corrected if the agent hears what the human did not understand. The run before this feature: a step row offered hide code and delete; nothing recorded a question for the agent and no Console command stored text for later, so the human retyped 'anchor, step 1.1.1, pin_span map.rs:819' and the question into Claude Code, and for code outside a tour needed a file:line they cannot copy out of Source; questions met mid-read were held in the head or lost. edit-tour helps only when the human already knows the answer. Writing the question into a step note with edit-tour puts it in .codemap/: it is committed, it merges with the agent's own edits to that note, and someone has to remember to delete it before the commit; a comment is a question, not documentation, so it lives outside the map and never reaches a commit. Comments are local to the machine, in the version control's shared directory so every jj workspace or git worktree of the repo sees them (the agent usually works in another workspace), and written at once rather than on ctrl+s, because the map's save is for edits the human may still discard while a question is meant for the agent now; a reply also reaches an open window with unsaved map edits, which reloading the map would not allow. Three designs were built and run: a Comments view listing every comment with a composer; a section of the References panel for the selection with badges on tree rows; and comments drawn inline where they are made. The view kept replies away from the code they answer, took height from References permanently and asked for the target with a radio list; the panel showed comments only for what is selected and only while References is open, and its target depended on the active tab. The inline design is the kept one, with the view's one useful part, finding every comment, moved into the count in the top bar (next-comment): a comment button on each step and tour header and 'comment on lines A-B' in the Source target strip; a one-line field opens in place, enter adds and escape cancels; the comment draws under the note or between the code lines it is on, orange while open, green with the agent's reply and dismiss once answered. The GUI scenario 'comments' adds one of each, answers one from the Console and dismisses it. The agent's side is the CLI: comments lists every open comment with its place, comment-reply answers one.

